---
export const prerender = true;

import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import LookupForm from '../components/LookupForm.astro';

const breadcrumbItems = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'IPv4 Guide', url: '/ipv4' },
];

const pageTitle = 'IPv4 Address: Architecture, Exhaustion, and Subnetting Guide | myipv4v6.com';
const pageDescription = 'Understand IPv4 32-bit address architecture, CIDR subnetting, RFC 1918 private ranges, NAT translation, and IPv4 exhaustion history.';
---

<Layout
  title={pageTitle}
  description={pageDescription}
  canonicalUrl="https://myipv4v6.com/ipv4"
>
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto w-full flex flex-col gap-10">
    <Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbItems} />

    <!-- Header -->
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-b border-neutral-200/80 dark:border-neutral-800 pb-8">
      <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-semibold bg-blue-50 text-blue-700 dark:bg-blue-950/70 dark:text-blue-300 w-fit">
        IPv4 Technical Reference
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-black tracking-tight text-neutral-950 dark:text-white">
        IPv4 (Internet Protocol Version 4)
      </h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed">
        The foundational networking protocol that built the modern internet. Learn how 32-bit addressing, octets, CIDR notation, and Network Address Translation (NAT) operate.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Search Tool -->
    <div class="rounded-3xl bg-neutral-100/80 dark:bg-neutral-900/60 border border-neutral-200/80 dark:border-neutral-800 p-6 sm:p-8">
      <h2 class="text-base font-bold text-neutral-900 dark:text-white mb-2">Test an IPv4 Address</h2>
      <LookupForm placeholder="Enter IPv4 address (e.g. 1.1.1.1 or 8.8.8.8)" />
    </div>

    <!-- Guide Content -->
    <article class="flex flex-col gap-8 text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300 leading-relaxed">
      <section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-950 dark:text-white">1. How IPv4 Addressing Works</h2>
        <p>
          Internet Protocol Version 4 (IPv4) was deployed in 1983 for ARPANET and defined in <strong>RFC 791</strong>. An IPv4 address is a 32-bit numeric value represented as four decimal numbers (each ranging from 0 to 255) separated by dots. Each number is called an <em>octet</em> because it represents 8 binary bits:
        </p>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-2xl bg-neutral-900 text-neutral-100 font-mono text-sm overflow-x-auto my-2">
          Dotted Decimal: 192 . 0 . 2 . 1<br/>
          Binary Format:  11000000 . 00000000 . 00000010 . 00000001
        </div>
        <p>
          Because IPv4 is 32 bits wide, the total address space is exactly 2<sup>32</sup>, or <strong>4,294,967,296 unique addresses</strong>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-950 dark:text-white">2. Private IPv4 Ranges (RFC 1918)</h2>
        <p>
          To conserve address space, specific blocks were reserved exclusively for private local networks. These addresses cannot be routed over the public internet:
        </p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto my-2">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-neutral-200 dark:border-neutral-800 text-neutral-900 dark:text-white font-bold">
                <th class="py-2.5 pr-4">IP Range</th>
                <th class="py-2.5 pr-4">CIDR Prefix</th>
                <th class="py-2.5 pr-4">Total Addresses</th>
                <th class="py-2.5">Typical Use Case</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-neutral-100 dark:divide-neutral-800/80">
              <tr>
                <td class="py-2.5 font-mono text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400">10.0.0.0 – 10.255.255.255</td>
                <td class="py-2.5 font-mono">10.0.0.0/8</td>
                <td class="py-2.5">16,777,216</td>
                <td class="py-2.5">Large enterprise & cloud VPC networks</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="py-2.5 font-mono text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400">172.16.0.0 – 172.31.255.255</td>
                <td class="py-2.5 font-mono">172.16.0.0/12</td>
                <td class="py-2.5">1,048,576</td>
                <td class="py-2.5">Medium corporate networks & Docker</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="py-2.5 font-mono text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400">192.168.0.0 – 192.168.255.255</td>
                <td class="py-2.5 font-mono">192.168.0.0/16</td>
                <td class="py-2.5">65,536</td>
                <td class="py-2.5">Home Wi-Fi routers & small offices</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="py-2.5 font-mono text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400">127.0.0.1 – 127.255.255.255</td>
                <td class="py-2.5 font-mono">127.0.0.0/8</td>
                <td class="py-2.5">16,777,216</td>
                <td class="py-2.5">Loopback (localhost testing)</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-950 dark:text-white">3. IPv4 Exhaustion & The Role of NAT</h2>
        <p>
          On February 3, 2011, the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA) officially allocated the last unassigned /8 address blocks. Regional registries subsequently exhausted their free pools:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1.5 text-sm sm:text-base">
          <li><strong>APNIC (Asia-Pacific):</strong> April 15, 2011</li>
          <li><strong>RIPE NCC (Europe):</strong> September 14, 2012</li>
          <li><strong>ARIN (North America):</strong> September 24, 2015</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The internet survived this exhaustion through widespread deployment of <strong>Carrier-Grade NAT (CGNAT)</strong> and the global rollout of <strong>IPv6</strong>.
        </p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>
